  <!-- Shell-firing weapons defer to the shell, revived in #145.

       A shotgun never asked its ammunition what to fire. MagazineAmmoLoader.HandleEvent(LoadAmmoEvent)
       consults the ammo's own ProjectileObject ONLY when the weapon leaves its own blank, and all four
       weapons that take a shell hardcoded a pellet - so every effect shell in Ammo.xml was inert, which
       is the "degraded to plain ammo" symptom recorded in #14.

       An empty ProjectileObject is vanilla's own way of saying "fire whatever is loaded". It appears
       exactly three times in the game - the Grenade Launcher and the Dart Gun, both weapons whose
       ammunition carries the payload - and the merge chain applies it verbatim: ObjectBlueprintXMLChildNode
       .Merge assigns attributes as written, and GamePartBlueprint builds a setter for the empty string
       with no skip, so the field really is blanked rather than left inherited.

       Plain Shotgun Shell is unaffected. ProjectileShotgunShell, ProjectileShotgunPellet and
       ProjectileCombatShotgunPellet are the same object three times over - BasePenetration 4 over 1d2,
       all inheriting BaseShotgunProjectile - so ordinary shot behaves identically before and after. The
       weapon's damage readout improves as a side effect: with the field empty, MagazineAmmoLoader's
       GetDisplayNameEvent reads the performance of the shell actually loaded.

       The cost, stated plainly: if Freehold ever differentiates the combat shotgun's pellet from the
       pump's, this merge discards it for anyone running the mod. That is charter rule 1's own failure
       mode, accepted deliberately here because the alternative is a whole category of ammunition that
       cannot exist. Both vanilla pellet blueprints are left declared and untouched.

       Our two shotguns are edited in place above rather than merged, since they are ours.
       Raven_ProjectileDrumShotgunPellet and Raven_ProjectileHandCannonMod are kept declared: they are
       now unreferenced, but deleting a shipped blueprint is a save-compatibility question of its own
       and they cost nothing where they stand. -->

  <object Name="Pump Shotgun" Load="Merge">
    <part Name="MagazineAmmoLoader" ProjectileObject="" AmmoPart="AmmoShotgunShell" MaxAmmo="1" />
  </object>

  <object Name="Combat Shotgun" Load="Merge">
